// **The conflict rule.** When an annotation and a call-site turbofish disagree, the TURBOFISH wins
// the instantiation and the disagreement is reported as an ordinary assignability error at the
// binding (E0007), pointing at the initializer.
//
// Chosen over a dedicated "these two type arguments disagree" diagnostic because it is the truthful
// account of what happened rather than a special case bolted on: `Repo::<Todo>.new("todos")` is a
// self-sufficient expression of type `Repo<Todo>`, exactly like `5` is an `int`, and `r: Repo<User>
// = <a Repo<Todo>>` is the same mistake as `n: int = "s"` — reported by the same rule, at the same
// span, with the same wording the rest of the language uses. A bespoke error would have to explain
// a precedence the reader now does not need to know.
//
// The precedence falls out of the machinery rather than being enforced: the class type arguments
// seed the substitution first, and `bind_type_params` binds with a first-wins `or_insert`, so the
// expected type can only fill what the turbofish left open. Nothing has to decide a winner.
// expect: error E0007 at 30:17
// expect: exit 1
struct Todo {
    id: int
}
struct User {
    id: int
}
class Repo<T> {
    pub tbl: string

    pub fn new(tbl: string): Repo<T> {
        return Repo { tbl: tbl };
    }
}
r: Repo<User> = Repo::<Todo>.new("todos");
echo r.tbl;
